WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:05.560
Okay, so now what is exactly left?

00:05.560 --> 00:06.440
Join this type.

00:06.440 --> 00:12.320
Gunner returns all the rows from the left table and only the matching from the right table.

00:12.320 --> 00:16.920
So now if you look again to our two circles A and B, what do we need from the left table.

00:16.920 --> 00:20.520
We want to see everything all the rows, all the data.

00:20.520 --> 00:22.800
So that means we will get a full circle.

00:22.840 --> 00:26.680
And now from the right table we want to get only the matching data.

00:26.680 --> 00:32.000
So that means we don't want to see everything from the table B we want to see only the records that

00:32.000 --> 00:37.640
has matched to the table A so that means, my friends, the left table has here more priority.

00:37.640 --> 00:41.040
This is the primary source of your data, the main source.

00:41.040 --> 00:42.440
We cannot miss anything.

00:42.440 --> 00:43.440
This is very important.

00:43.440 --> 00:45.520
We want to see all the data from the table.

00:45.560 --> 00:50.800
B it is a secondary source of data and we are joining it only to get an additional data.

00:50.800 --> 00:51.880
So I don't want everything.

00:51.880 --> 00:55.280
I want only the data that has matched to the left table.

00:55.280 --> 00:57.400
So this is what we mean with a left join.

00:57.440 --> 01:00.880
Now if you look to the syntax it's going to be very similar to the inner join.

01:00.880 --> 01:04.630
So we start from the left table the A, then we say left join.

01:04.670 --> 01:07.590
The right table B and then the same condition using keys.

01:07.630 --> 01:10.670
So here we just switch the type instead of inner.

01:10.670 --> 01:11.630
We have now left.

01:11.630 --> 01:14.710
But now here with the syntax we need to be very careful.

01:14.750 --> 01:17.390
The order of the tables now is very important.

01:17.390 --> 01:19.510
You have to start from the correct table.

01:19.510 --> 01:23.350
So you have to mention the left table exactly in the From clause.

01:23.390 --> 01:25.910
And then you join it with the right table.

01:25.910 --> 01:28.750
So in the join you have to specify the right table.

01:28.750 --> 01:33.950
If you don't do it like this then you will not get all the data from A and you will not get the results

01:33.950 --> 01:35.110
that you are expecting.

01:35.110 --> 01:36.990
So this is what we mean with a left join.

01:37.030 --> 01:39.230
Let's go back to SQL in order to practice.

01:39.230 --> 01:39.590
All right.

01:39.630 --> 01:40.870
So now we have the following task.

01:40.870 --> 01:46.830
It says get all customers along with their orders including those without orders.

01:46.830 --> 01:51.070
So again here we need the data from two tables the customers and orders.

01:51.070 --> 01:52.710
And we want everything in one result.

01:52.710 --> 01:54.310
That means we have to go and join the data.

01:54.310 --> 01:57.910
And now the task says includes those without orders.

01:57.910 --> 02:04.070
So that means I want to see everything, the matching data and the unmatching data from the table Customers.

02:04.070 --> 02:08.270
And by looking to our query, this is not working because we are not getting everything right.

02:08.270 --> 02:12.110
We are getting only the customers that has match in the table orders.

02:12.110 --> 02:14.430
And this is not, of course, fulfilling the task.

02:14.470 --> 02:18.070
So now if you read the task you can understand the main table.

02:18.110 --> 02:19.430
Here is the customers.

02:19.430 --> 02:23.350
We are not speaking about to see all the orders and not missing any order.

02:23.350 --> 02:26.110
And the orders here is only for additional information.

02:26.110 --> 02:31.710
So now, in order to not lose any data for the customers, we make sure we start from the table customers.

02:31.710 --> 02:34.190
So that means now the customers on the left side.

02:34.230 --> 02:36.870
And now after that instead of inner join.

02:36.870 --> 02:37.910
This is not good thing.

02:37.910 --> 02:39.990
For this task we are going to say left join.

02:39.990 --> 02:43.630
And with that we guarantee we will get all the data from the customers.

02:43.670 --> 02:47.870
Now we say left join orders and of course the condition can stay like this.

02:47.870 --> 02:50.110
This is how we are connecting the two tables.

02:50.110 --> 02:51.470
So actually that's it.

02:51.470 --> 02:53.030
Let's go and execute it.

02:53.070 --> 02:57.110
And now by looking to the result you can see that we have now five customers.

02:57.110 --> 02:59.750
Even the customers that didn't place any orders.

02:59.750 --> 03:01.670
So you can see Martin and Peter.

03:01.710 --> 03:06.140
They don't have any order ID so that means they didn't order anything.

03:06.180 --> 03:09.980
And as you can see, skill is showing us nulls when there is no match.

03:09.980 --> 03:11.540
So with that, we have solved the task.

03:11.540 --> 03:13.180
Now, my friends, one more thing.

03:13.220 --> 03:18.860
As I told you, the order of the tables is very important because the customer is now the left table

03:18.900 --> 03:22.540
because you start from it and the second table, the orders is the right table.

03:22.580 --> 03:25.420
Now if you go and switch them like this.

03:25.420 --> 03:30.260
So we start from the orders and then join it with the customers and you go execute it.

03:30.260 --> 03:32.100
You will not get all the customers.

03:32.100 --> 03:34.140
And of course the task is now not solved.

03:34.140 --> 03:38.620
So as you can see, you are getting now completely different results if you go and switch the tables.

03:38.620 --> 03:44.220
So be careful where you start and how you join the tables in order to get the effects that you want.

03:44.260 --> 03:44.660
All right.

03:44.660 --> 03:47.060
So now I'm going to put everything back like before.

03:47.100 --> 03:51.460
Now let's go and understand how SQL exactly executed this query okay.

03:51.500 --> 03:54.140
So now again we have the data from customers and orders.

03:54.140 --> 03:55.860
And this time we are doing the left join.

03:55.860 --> 03:57.580
So now let's see how it's going to do it.

03:57.580 --> 04:00.460
So I'm going to say okay we need the ID and the first name.

04:00.500 --> 04:02.820
And we will get that as well in the results.

04:02.820 --> 04:08.420
And from the right table we need only those two informations the order ID and the sales in the output.

04:08.420 --> 04:10.260
So those are the columns that we need.

04:10.260 --> 04:13.100
So now SQL in the left join can do it a little bit differently.

04:13.100 --> 04:16.140
It can start as well from the left table from the customers.

04:16.140 --> 04:22.900
But this time it's going to go and immediately put the result in the output without like trying to match

04:22.900 --> 04:26.740
anything and to check whether the data exists or not, because it doesn't matter.

04:26.780 --> 04:32.340
SQL not doing any validation whether the customer exists in the orders since it's left join is going

04:32.340 --> 04:34.300
to show all the data from the left table.

04:34.300 --> 04:36.100
So there will be like no check.

04:36.100 --> 04:40.820
But now as a next step in order to get the order ID and the sales SQL will start searching.

04:40.820 --> 04:43.340
So SQL can go over here and start searching.

04:43.340 --> 04:45.660
Where do we have a customer with this ID?

04:45.660 --> 04:47.380
Well, it's going to be the first order.

04:47.380 --> 04:51.460
We're going to get the order ID and as well the sales informations.

04:51.460 --> 04:53.380
And we will see that in the output.

04:53.380 --> 04:54.980
So that's it for the first one.

04:54.980 --> 04:56.580
Now it's going to go to the second row.

04:56.580 --> 04:58.860
And the same thing going to happen immediately.

04:58.860 --> 05:03.140
The SQL is going to go and put the result in the output without checking anything.

05:03.180 --> 05:08.060
And then in order to get the order data it will start searching for this ID.

05:08.060 --> 05:08.170
idea.

05:08.170 --> 05:09.890
So we have it here in the second row.

05:09.930 --> 05:11.930
We have the order ID and the sales.

05:11.970 --> 05:14.690
And it's going to put those results to the outputs.

05:14.730 --> 05:16.970
So that's it for the third one.

05:17.010 --> 05:22.490
Immediately going to put everything in the output and then start searching for orders.

05:22.490 --> 05:25.050
With this ID we have it over here.

05:25.050 --> 05:27.930
So this order belongs to the user ID number three.

05:27.970 --> 05:30.610
So far we are getting the same result as the inner join.

05:30.610 --> 05:31.730
But we are not done yet.

05:31.770 --> 05:33.290
Now exactly count the difference.

05:33.330 --> 05:38.930
It's going to go and get Martin and put it immediately in the output and start searching for an order

05:38.930 --> 05:39.770
with this ID.

05:39.770 --> 05:42.610
So do we have any order with the ID number four?

05:42.770 --> 05:45.130
Well, we don't have anything this time.

05:45.130 --> 05:48.730
SQL of course will not go and exclude the ID number four.

05:48.770 --> 05:49.650
It's going to leave it.

05:49.650 --> 05:53.970
But in SQL if there is no match we still have to have something in the output.

05:54.010 --> 05:59.210
So it's going to go and say the output going to be null like this.

05:59.210 --> 05:59.970
We don't know.

05:59.970 --> 06:00.730
It is unknown.

06:00.770 --> 06:02.730
And the same thing for the sales.

06:02.730 --> 06:05.730
So in the left join if there is no match you will see nulls.

06:05.850 --> 06:08.170
Same thing for the next customer for better.

06:08.290 --> 06:14.250
So SQL will go and put the result immediately in the output and then start searching the orders.

06:14.290 --> 06:16.650
So do we have anything for the ID number five?

06:16.690 --> 06:18.250
We don't have anything.

06:18.250 --> 06:22.730
That's why SQL is going to go and present nulls as well in the outputs.

06:22.730 --> 06:27.690
And that's why you saw nulls in the output because those customers don't have any orders.

06:27.690 --> 06:29.770
So this is exactly the effect of the left join.

06:29.770 --> 06:34.290
You will get everything from the left table and only the matching stuff on the right side.

06:34.290 --> 06:36.890
And if there is something not matching, you will get nulls.

06:36.890 --> 06:37.730
So that is.

06:37.730 --> 06:40.250
This is how SQL execute the left join.

06:40.290 --> 06:42.530
Okay, so now back to this use cases of joins.

06:42.530 --> 06:48.250
If I think about left join I can use it in order to recombine data in order to build this big picture.

06:48.250 --> 06:53.490
And as well in the second use case where we use it in order to get an extra information from another

06:53.490 --> 06:53.890
table.

06:53.890 --> 06:56.450
So we have a main table and a secondary table.

06:56.450 --> 06:58.410
So we use it for both use cases.

06:58.410 --> 07:02.650
And as well in the third use case only with a twist that we're going to learn later.

07:02.650 --> 07:04.290
So that's all about the left join.

07:04.330 --> 07:08.210
Now we have another type that is exactly the opposite of the left join.

07:08.210 --> 07:09.930
We have the right join.

07:09.930 --> 07:11.930
So now let's understand what this means.
